study_outside:
Spend 1-2 hours to read/watch relevant materials before the class;
Finish the assignments after the class.
【Tips】
1.This course is the most fundamental and practical one among all the courses I teach. After systematically learning a range of skills in anthropological methods, it will be possible to turn your curiosity into a real research;
2.This course will be taught in English only, so it welcomes all students to choose it and looks forward to your research topics that are relevant to your cultures.

1.This course will provide a large range of literature on Chinese study, so we hope that each student will choose their own area of interest to read in depth. It will take 2-3 hours at least to scan and read relevant materials (including books and papers) before and after the class and each student should note areas of confusion so that these topics can be raised in the lesson;
2.As a CLIL course in my APM course, this course is not only suitable for students who want to go to China for research or investigation in the future, but also welcome all students who are interested in China, a huge country with such an ancient civilization. Rather than involve much Chinese language teaching, the course will focus on the Chinese context. This course will be conducted bilingually and will engage in cross-cultural discussions of concepts that are unique to Chinese and difficult to translate. It is primarily designed for students with an intermediate or higher level of Chinese proficiency. Students with more limited Chinese proficiency who are interested in the course are welcome to attend a trial session before making a final decision about enrollment.
Preview and Review may take around 90-120 min. each.

summary:
This course is designed for complete beginners in French who want to acquire a solid competence in French during their undergraduate studies, and possibly study-abroad in a French-speaking country (France, Switzerland, Belgium, Canada,Africa...) either at undergraduate (Sciences Po, Geneva University, Brussels University, Montreal University...) or graduate level (Sciences Po Paris,Graduate Institute of International and Development Studies of Geneva...). Ideally, students should minimally graduate as trilingual (Japanese/English/French) citizens in order to work or study further on the international scene. To do so, students enrolling in the Intensive French course are strongly advised to enroll at the same time in other relevant courses offered in the SILS French-related program - refer to the syllabus for more detailed information. The Intensive French Ⅰ course includes 4 classes per week: all 4 classes are compulsory (you cannot skip some of the classes). The textbook used for this class is published in France (Inspire 1, see:
https://www.hachettefle.com/collections/inspire
) and is essentially based on the principles of the communicative approaches. Each of its lessons includes dialogues and activities (role-plays as well as grammar exercises, reading and listening tasks) covering a wide range of“know-how” (e.g. ask a question, accept or refuse something, talk about yourself, etc.), lexical, grammatical and phonetic content. It also contains online resources for students to use in class and outside of class. Students are expected to be active in class and to use French as much as possible. The teachers may teach in French, English or Japanese, depending on the needs of the students. The textbook content covered in class is the same as the one in French classes, but with more time to learn, practice in class and strengthen knowledge and skills. At the end of the semester, students should have learned enough vocabulary to manage to communicate in daily life, using simple French expressions (Target: Level A1 in the CFRL)

